WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.180
Film Courage: I’m not sure if this relates
to contrast but to say that the audience loves

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.839
contrast, I think you talked about it in one
of your videos.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:10.770
If a character comes in [to a scene] angry,
then they need to leave with some resolve

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.760
or happy (something).

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.139
But if they come in happy, then they need
to leave the scene angry there needs to be

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:17.310
some kind of a contrast.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:23.220
Adam Skelter: Yeah that’s a principle I
learned from you know just reading Robert

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:30.109
McKee basically that the idea is that a scene
turns on the emotional state of the character

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:37.790
or characters and basically the idea is that
by contrast the whole point of story is to

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:46.560
get us to engage emotionally so when we’re
tracking something the logical should be as

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.110
it should be as logical as it needs to be
so that we can emotionally invest in something

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:58.270
so something needs to be logical if it within
the confines of the world just enough so that

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:02.610
we can care about what they care about because
what we really are interested in is what is

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.460
the emotional state of these characters.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:13.340
So the best way to know if the scene has turned
is if this character comes in with this positive

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.859
emotion, they experience some conflict and
the results causes a shift.

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:25.819
If they come in happy and generally speaking
nothing significant has happened.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:29.680
And so what we’re looking for when we structure
our scenes (what I’m looking for when I

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:38.479
structure the scenes is some sort of emotionally
significant experience changes the way they

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.009
are seeing their situation.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:46.049
And then from there using their strategy they
adapt to new conflict.

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:50.079
Film Courage: Is that the one where you use
GLENGARRY GLEN ROSS?

00:01:50.079 --> 00:02:00.279
Adam: You know Mamet’s creed is what does
the character want, why now and what happens

00:02:00.279 --> 00:02:01.340
if they don’t get it?

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.810
So it’s asking those three questions which
form a kind of litmus test to whether the

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.780
scene is worth it.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:15.799
And then from there you take McKee’s principle
of turning a positive to a negative or a negative

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:22.650
to a positive and the idea of contrast is
what I’m trying to say is that you want

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:29.120
to pace out a film and it shouldn’t be just
ridiculous jumps and contrasts.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.560
Like if a main character comes in and she’s
just gloating with joy and then it’s the

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.080
depths of sorrow that’s melodrama and we
begin to disengage.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.610
I mean there are plenty of exceptions to it.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:52.360
But ultimately what we are looking for is
a kind of journey which means we’re following

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.240
the emotional journey that they are going
through where we’re following the ups and

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.020
downs and usually some shifts and then sometimes
it drops out from under you.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.890
So a lot of that is about understanding the
way you can pace out and establish a rhythm

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.280
of different scenes and you start to develop
certain stakes and then you drop it and raise

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.120
the stakes and things like that.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.340
So it’s all about playing with the expectations
and getting the audience to guess what’s

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:14.340
going to happen next.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.610
Film Courage: So are those the three questions
you are asking characters, even supporting

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:20.610
characters?

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.819
Not just the protagonist or the antagonist
just some of the supporting ones?

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:33.140
Adam: Yes, so a lot of things about structure
especially is largely to do with rewriting

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.739
although it does inform outlining as well.

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:38.050
That’s all the principles inform each other.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.379
A lot of the story structure theory, like
for example especially scenes dynamics, that

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:46.890
video was largely due to kind of almost like
checklist once you’ve already written a

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:51.790
scene, these are some ways to know if it’s
working or not.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:59.050
So I took Mamet’s creed and I took Robert
McKee’s principle of turning from the emotions

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:03.999
up to positive to negative and vice versa
and then I can up with a list of essential

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:09.770
scene dynamics and a lot of that, it’s kind
of like a checklist of something that I’ll

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:15.689
be reading something, I’ll make sure that
each scene that I write has these elements

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:22.770
and if they don’t have the elements then
usually I can diagnose exactly what’s wrong

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.000
with it, what’s not working with it.

